# R1 — Shared modules only (in inspector pages)

Inspector pages are **windows** onto the real system, not parallel implementations.
Any logic that does real work (LLM calls, graph queries, world-API calls,
RC transport, PCG parameterisation) lives in `packages/`. Inspector pages
import that logic and render its inputs/outputs — nothing more.

If you find yourself writing business logic inside `apps/inspector/`, **stop**:
extract it to the correct `packages/*` module first, then import it.

## ❌ BAD

```ts
// apps/inspector/src/pages/03-world-api-mock-bench.tsx
function callTool(name: string, args: unknown) {
  // ad-hoc fetch to /rc/preset — production uses a different code path
  return fetch(`/rc/preset/${name}`, { method: "PUT", body: JSON.stringify(args) });
}
```

## ✅ GOOD

```ts
// apps/inspector/src/pages/03-world-api-mock-bench.tsx
import type { WorldAPIClient } from "@yellow-ue/world-api";
import { MockWorldAPIClient } from "@yellow-ue/world-api/mock";

const client: WorldAPIClient = new MockWorldAPIClient();
const result = await client.setSkyState({ preset: "storm" });
```

## How to fix a violation

1. Identify what the inspector is doing that should live elsewhere.
2. Move it into the appropriate `packages/*` module (creating one if needed).
3. Re-import it in the inspector.
4. If no package fits, that's a signal a new boundary is being born — see R5.
